Lab 02 - Ubuntu server VM with firewall setup

Goal: Build an ubuntu server VM and use it to practice enabling firewalls (Uncomplicated Firewall - ufw) and
transfer files via sftp and rsync.

Setup

Host: HP Elitebook 8460p laptop running Ubuntu 24.04 LTS
VM software: Oracle VirtualBox
Guest: Ubuntu Server 26.04.1 LTS
Network: NAT with a port-forward rule (host: tugy host ip: 127.0.0.1: host port: 2222 to guest port: 22)

What I did:

- Create a snapshot (pre firewall) before enabling the ufw
- Check ufw status
- Set the ufw default policies [deny incoming and allow outgoing]
- Allow ssh on port 22/tcp
- Check the ufw rules again to make sure that the default policies are added especially
ssh on port 22/tcp
- Enable firewall
- Confirmed if firewall was up and active
- Open a brand new sftp connection and tried rsync to see if the ufw allows ssh on port 22


Results

- Sftp worked and connected for file transfer
- Rsync also worked

What I learned

- ssh must be enabled and not inactive(dead)
- After checking the status of ufw and it is not enabled, you set the default policies first
    - sudo ufw default deny incoming - to block all incoming connections except allowed by a rule
    - sudo ufw default allow outgoing - to allow all outgoing connections freely
- After setting the default policies, you open only ssh on port 22 so that it can be connected to
    - sudo ufw allow 22/tcp
- After allowing ssh on port 22, you can now enable the ufw and this is done so that you don't lock yourself out ( I still don't understand this technically but I'll come back to it)

